      ],
      "budget_share": 0.4,
      "why": "Registering every m stages trades some area (fewer FFs) for a longer combinational path; m=2-4 lets the optimizer find the area/Fmax sweet spot that still clears 1 MSPS, mapping the area-accuracy trade-off curve."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec demands >=1 MSPS and <=2^-10 error with area as the primary objective. Only pipelined and pipelined_m produce 1 result/cycle, so throughput is governed by Fmax; both are viable. I focus on W=12-16 and N=10-14 because the 2^-10 error bound is tight enough that very small N/W will fail, but large N/W wastes area. Rounding is fixed to 'round' to maximize accuracy per bit, leaving the budget to explore the area/accuracy trade-off. The fully pipelined family gets the larger share since it has the shortest critical path (best Fmax per area), while pipelined_m with m=2-4 explores the area-reduction axis by removing some registers at the cost of a longer path."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The Pareto front is well-established and feasible. The minimum area design (pipelined_m, 19953 um^2) meets all constraints (throughput 43.5 MSPS >= 1, max_abs_err 0.000661 <= 0.000977). The front covers a reasonable range of accuracy (10.6 to 12.3 bits) against the reference (10 bits) and area (19953 to 39385 um^2) against the reference (40000 um^2). Since this is the final round, further exploration is not possible. The current front provides a clear trade-off curve for the selection rule (min area), which would pick the 19953 um^2 design."
,
